// gurmukhi-demo — Gurmukhi (Punjabi) script typesetting in texish.
//
// Render with:  texish-cli scripts/gurmukhi-demo.script
//
// Gurmukhi is written left to right, so it needs none of the bidirectional
// reordering Hebrew and Arabic do. What it needs instead is work inside each
// syllable. A consonant carries an inherent vowel; a following vowel sign (a
// matra) attaches above, below, before or after it; and a consonant joined to
// the next by the virama (U+0A4D) takes a subjoined form beneath the base.
//
// The engine segments the run into orthographic syllables, then for each one asks
// the bundled Noto Serif Gurmukhi font's GSUB table to build the subjoined forms
// and reorders the sihari — the i-sign, typed after its consonant but drawn before
// the whole syllable. The below- and above-base signs, the nasal tippi and the
// gemination addak are then placed with the same GPOS anchors the engine uses for
// Hebrew points and Arabic dots. Unlike Devanagari and Bengali, Gurmukhi has no
// reph: a joined ra hangs below its base as the subjoined pairin, it never rises
// as a mark above the syllable.
//
// \font gurmukhi selects the bundled face (also \font punjabi), in a regular and a
// bold cut. No \rtl is needed — the script is left to right.

\use{document}

\title{The Gurmukhi Script in \TeXish}
\author{The texish engine}
\maketitle

\section{Setting a Gurmukhi paragraph}

Each syllable is shaped on its own and the syllables are set left to right. Within a
syllable the consonants may take subjoined forms and the vowel signs attach around the
base; between syllables the line breaker finds its breaks at the interword spaces exactly
as for Latin text.

\medskip

{\font gurmukhi 14 regular
ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਇੱਕ ਅਮੀਰ ਭਾਸ਼ਾ ਹੈ। ਇਹ ਖੱਬੇ ਤੋਂ ਸੱਜੇ ਲਿਖੀ ਜਾਂਦੀ ਹੈ। ਹਰ ਅੱਖਰ ਇੱਕ ਵਿਅੰਜਨ ਅਤੇ ਉਸ ਦੇ
ਨਾਲ ਦਾ ਸਵਰ ਮਿਲ ਕੇ ਬਣਦਾ ਹੈ, ਅਤੇ ਦੋ ਵਿਅੰਜਨ ਵਿਰਾਮ ਨਾਲ ਜੁੜ ਕੇ ਸੰਯੁਕਤ ਰੂਪ ਬਣਾਉਂਦੇ ਹਨ।
}

\section{The pre-base sihari}

The i-sign, the sihari, is typed after its consonant but drawn before it. In each word
below the sign appears at the left of its syllable even though it follows the consonant
in memory --- day, father, and written:

\medskip

{\font gurmukhi 16 regular
ਦਿਨ   ਪਿਤਾ   ਲਿਖਿਆ
}

\section{Subjoined consonants}

A consonant joined to the next by the virama takes a subjoined form beneath the base ---
the pairin. The joined ra, ha and va hang below their base, as in love, holy, and sign:

\medskip

{\font gurmukhi 16 regular
ਪ੍ਰੇਮ   ਸ੍ਰੀ   ਚਿੰਨ੍ਹ
}

\section{Addak and the nasals}

The addak doubles the consonant that follows it, and the tippi and bindi mark a nasal.
They are placed as marks over the base, as in one, true, Punjab, and colour:

\medskip

{\font gurmukhi 16 regular
ਇੱਕ   ਸੱਚ   ਪੰਜਾਬ   ਰੰਗ
}

\section{Independent vowels and signs}

A vowel that opens a syllable is written with its own independent letter, not a matra.
The below- and above-base signs stack onto their base:

\medskip

{\font gurmukhi 16 regular
ਅ ਆ ਇ ਈ ਉ ਊ ਏ ਐ ਓ ਔ
}

\medskip

{\font gurmukhi 16 regular
ਕਾ ਕਿ ਕੀ ਕੁ ਕੂ ਕੇ ਕੈ ਕੋ ਕੌ
}

\section{A closing line}

The traditional Sikh greeting, set in the bold cut:

\medskip

{\font gurmukhi 18 bold
ਸਤਿ ਸ੍ਰੀ ਅਕਾਲ! ਇਹ ਗੁਰਮੁਖੀ ਲਿਪੀ ਵਿੱਚ ਲਿਖਿਆ ਹੈ।
}
